A flexible pipe connector (200) for joining a pressurised mains pipe to a service pipe, the connector comprising:

- a corrugated longitudinal body portion (202) having a first end (208), a second end (210), and an internal bore extending therebetween, said longitudinal body portion further comprising a plurality of corrugations spaced along its length, the corrugations defining a maximum internal diameter of the bore at the peak (204) of a corrugation and a minimum internal diameter of the bore at the trough (206) of a corrugation;

- a first spigot socket (212) disposed at said first end of the longitudinal body portion; and

- a second spigot socket (214) disposed at said second end of the longitudinal body portion;

- wherein, for a given volumetric flow rate, the head loss of fluid flowing through said body portion is no greater than that which would occur in a smooth-bored pipe of equivalent length and having the same internal diameter as the first and second spigot sockets; and

- wherein the minimum internal diameter of said corrugated internal bore exceeds the maximum internal diameter of said first and second spigot sockets.

outputs.L2:
A bendable pipe coupling device (200) designed to connect a pressurized water main to a service line, comprising the following elements:

• An elongated, ribbed central section (202) extending between a first terminus (208) and a second terminus (210), with a hollow passage running through it. This central section incorporates multiple ridges distributed along its length, where each ridge's crest (204) establishes the widest point of the internal passage, while each ridge's valley (206) establishes the narrowest point of the internal passage;

• A first spigot fitting (212) positioned at the initial terminus of the elongated central section; and

• A second spigot fitting (214) positioned at the opposite terminus of the elongated central section;

• Such that, at any given flow volume, fluid passing through the central section experiences pressure loss no greater than what would occur in a straight-walled pipe having identical length and diameter matching that of the spigot fittings; and

• Such that the narrowest interior width of the ribbed passage exceeds the widest exterior width of both spigot fittings.

