# Independent Patent Claim

**Claim 1.** A flexible corrugated pipe connector for fluidly coupling a pressurized water main to a service pipe, the connector comprising:

an elongate tubular body defining a fluid flow passage extending along a longitudinal axis between a first end and a second end, the tubular body comprising a corrugated intermediate section disposed between the first end and the second end, the corrugated intermediate section comprising a plurality of alternating annular ridges and grooves formed integrally about a circumference of the tubular body, the corrugated intermediate section being configured to permit angular deflection, axial compression, and axial extension of the tubular body relative to the longitudinal axis so as to accommodate misalignment and relative movement between the water main and the service pipe while maintaining the fluid flow passage in a sealed condition under an internal pressurization of the pressurized water main;

a first spigot socket integrally formed at the first end of the tubular body, the first spigot socket comprising a first substantially cylindrical, non-corrugated coupling surface configured for sealed insertion into or engagement with a corresponding fitting associated with the water main; and

a second spigot socket integrally formed at the second end of the tubular body, the second spigot socket comprising a second substantially cylindrical, non-corrugated coupling surface configured for sealed insertion into or engagement with a corresponding fitting associated with the service pipe;

wherein each of the first spigot socket and the second spigot socket comprises a sealing arrangement disposed thereon and configured to form a fluid-tight seal against the respective fitting sufficient to maintain sealing integrity of the fluid flow passage against the internal pressurization of the pressurized water main during the angular deflection, axial compression, or axial extension of the corrugated intermediate section.

# Patent Claim

**Claim 1:**

A flexible pipe coupling comprising:

a corrugated tubular body defining a longitudinal axis and having a plurality of annular corrugations formed along its length, the corrugated body being configured to permit angular deflection and axial flexibility between adjoining pipe sections while maintaining fluid-tight integrity;

a first spigot socket integrally formed at a first end of the corrugated tubular body, the first spigot socket configured to sealingly engage a first pipe section;

a second spigot socket integrally formed at a second end of the corrugated tubular body, opposite the first end, the second spigot socket configured to sealingly engage a second pipe section;

wherein an internal bore of the corrugated tubular body is dimensioned and configured such that a hydraulic head loss resulting from fluid flow through the flexible pipe coupling does not exceed a hydraulic head loss produced by an equivalent length of smooth-bore pipe having a same nominal diameter and conveying an equivalent fluid flow rate under equivalent flow conditions;

wherein the corrugations of the corrugated tubular body are configured with a corrugation depth, corrugation pitch, and wall profile selected to minimize turbulence and flow resistance at the internal bore, such that the flexible pipe coupling maintains hydraulic performance substantially equivalent to that of the smooth-bore pipe while providing said angular deflection and axial flexibility between the first pipe section and the second pipe section.
